So:

`this.get(n)` after == (`\old(this.get(n))` || `set.get(n)`)

But note: `set` is a parameter, and we are not modifying it. So `set.get(n)` refers to the state at the time of the call (not old, because `set` is not modified).

Also, we must ensure we don't assume bounds — the BitSet supports arbitrary non-negative indices.

Thus, the correct assertion is:

`\forall int n; n >= 0; this.get(n) == (\old(this.get(n)) || set.get(n))`

This asserts that for all non-negative bit indices `n`, the resulting bit is set iff it was set before in `this` or is set in `set`.

